{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-103687",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14263803/assets/d0f3e760be991c936baf5e9825cd582f086a73a8330032d24fd35fd5594b4864.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ has vertical asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\operatorname{sec}⁡0=1,$ the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ passes through the point $(0,1).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The graph of the function does not have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. Thus, $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ is not an odd function.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
